if statements
● Consider the fragment below.
if(x == 13) {
printf(“is it your lucky number?”);
}
● You know that the expression x == 13 will 
return true(1) or false(0). If it returns true, 
then the printf expression will be evaluated. 
Otherwise, it will not be evaluated. 
● According to the user input, you have two 
different programs. 
● Notice the curly braces and the indentation.

if statements
● Consider the fragment below
if(x == 13) { 
printf(“is it your lucky number?”);
}
● This piece of code is useful if you want to do 
something in only one case (in this case, if 
the user enters 13). 
● Let’s try a secret code screen.

TheCode.c

Stop… or else
● The user either enters the code correctly 
(true) or not (false).
● For such cases you can use else.
● Notice that the curly braces immediately 
follow the else keyword. 
● if (...) {
// run these statements if it is true
} else {
// run these statements if it is false
}

Here is a sample output

Which one to use?
● It depends on your programming task. 
● Actually, there is one more type of these if
statements where you can make it choose 
for more than two choices.
● For such cases, we use else if. Typically, for 
creating menus on our command-line 
programs.

else if
● It is really not that complicated:
if(...) {
// run if this expression is true
} else if (...) {
// run if this second expression is true
} else {
// run if none of them are true
}
● You can skip the last else. In that case, if none 
of the statements return true, all of them are 
skipped. 
● You can have as much else if as you require.

if inside an if? if it is possible, then...
● You can have another if statement in 
another if statement. We call them nested if 
statements. 
if (expression1) {
if(expression2) {
statements
}
}
● Of course, expression1 AND expression 2 
must both be true for statements to run. 
Wait. AND?

NESTED

What if?
● What if you enter 4 as input? 
● Homework: Write this program at home and 
explain why it gives different outputs.

Conditional Operator
● You will be using if and else a lot. In some 
cases, it will only be as follows:
if (expression1) { expression2; }
else { expression3; }
● Well, there is a conditional operator which 
keeps it shorter:
expression1 ? expression2 : expression3;

An example
if (x < 5) {
y = 5;
} else {
y = 10;
}
// this becomes:
y = (x < 5) ? 5 : 10; 
// more fun:
printf(“%d\n”, (x < 5) ? 5 : 10);

The switch statement
● There is a multiway conditional statement 
that generalizes the if-else if-else
statements. 
● switch (expression) {
case expr1: /* statement(s) */
case expr2: /* statement(s) */
/* as much case as you need */
default: /* if none of them match, switch 
uses this instead */
}
● There is a catch with the switch.

Moving along cases:
● int x = 0;
scanf(“%d”, &x);
switch(x) {
case 1:
printf(“entered 1”);
case 2:
printf(“entered 2”);
default: 
printf(“entered something else”);
}
● The switch evaluates x and if it is 1, it starts
evaluating from case 1. That means, it runs all 
the remaining (inc. default) as well. 
● If it is 2, it skips case 1 and starts from case 2.

switch is not the same as if
● So, switch is not the same as if. 
● However, we can make it run like if, if we 
want to. 
● We can use the keyword break to stop the 
cases when we are done. 
● The break keyword is also used in loops. 
● It causes an exit from the innermost (in this 
case it is nested) switch.
● Here is the favourite colour program using 
switch (with breaks of course).

switch
● So, why need switch? 
● It has its uses. Consider a problem where 
you need the program to jump to a line 
according to the input.
● See the code on the next slide.

switch put to good use

Using if or switch?
● You can use either if or a switch for a 
problem. 
● Always pay attention to your indentation.
● Also, as a good programming habit, make 
sure you make the most common case the 
first one.

